WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.320
[Narrator:] Bill Macalilly, 52 years old,

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:13.580
former naval officer once athletically inclined.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.390
Formerly a vigorous and active man.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.850
Today Bill finds himself winded after half a block's walk,

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:24.740
so short of breath he must stop to rest before continuing the rest of the way.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:26.360
By the time he reaches his car,

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:29.180
he is so out of breath that he needs a whiff of oxygen

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:36.850
to bring him back to normal.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.270
Frank Ussery, former construction worker,

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:42.290
spent his spare time hunting and fishing.

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:46.700
Today the act of shaving, a routine and effortless task to most men,

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:49.940
taxes him so that he has to stop frequently to catch his breath,

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.880
even though he sits and leans on the sink to conserve his energy.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:01:05.120
[Music]

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:07.400
Harry Krause, formerly a farmer,

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.280
has to go through this process four times each day

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.910
to clear his lungs of accumulated fluids

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:16.850
so he can lead a very limited existence comfortably.

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:21.150
These men share in common with an estimated 13 million Americans

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:26.390
a disabling disease that can make every breath of air they draw a major effort.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:31.010
They have emphysema.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:52.420
[Music]

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:55.110
[Narrator:] When emphysema strikes, the act of breathing,

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:58.740
an involuntary function to healthy people, becomes hard work,

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:02.110
sometimes demanding most of the strength the body can muster,

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.750
leaving its victims gasping for air.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:08.750
Emphysema victims often find themselves drowning in a sea of air,

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:13.130
unable to obtain the life-supporting oxygen their systems require.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:14.700
To Bill Maccalilly and his family,

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.420
this is an ever-present threat.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:21.750
[Bill Sr.'s wife:] Well, we'd been to the grocery store and I went out to the car,

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:25.420
it was about to rain and I picked him up at the door,

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.820
and we had two fairly heavy bags of groceries,

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.360
which he lifted into the car and...

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:31.380
I guess that was it,

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.100
it just took his breath completely,

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.520
and I had forgotten to put the little ball of oxygen we normally carry

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.140
into the car we drove out here,

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:39.260
and I thought I might have to run him

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.380
by the hospital before we got here.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.430
But he got his breath all right,

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:44.190
and we made it,

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:48.610
but that was one of the worst times I've seen him have lately.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:49.570
[Bill:] Mm hmm.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.060
[Bill Sr.'s wife:] Maybe it was the humidity.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:52.070
You never really know.

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:55.000
[Bill:] It musta been the air probably.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:58.800
It's real frightening not to be able to get your breath.

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:01.920
Uhh I have a tank, a tank at home,

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:05.520
I have a tank out here with a face mask and regulator.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:08.670
I have a ball of oxygen that I take in the car with me,

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:13.560
so that wherever I go I have access to the oxygen.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:15.540
And I don't need it every day,

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:19.030
but the fact that it's there gives me a self-assurance,

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:22.140
gives me confidence that if something should happen,

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:24.360
I have the oxygen available.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:29.730
And that does as much for me possibly as the oxygen itself.

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:31.760
[Bill Sr.'s wife:] And of course I've learned to live with,

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:33.280
we have learned to live with this,

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:39.300
but at first, I was scared to death frankly all the time uhhm.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:42.050
When Mack would leave the house,

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:44.990
I would be like this until he got back because I was just sure

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:47.480
he was going to die before he would get back.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:51.550
In fact I worried myself into a nice little ulcer

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:55.790
during that period but ummm, then gradually we learned

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:59.280
what emphysema was and how it affected a person

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:02.490
and what we had to do to learn to live with it.

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:07.940
So, I'd say we've improved considerably in that direction, too.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:10.690
[Bill:] I look big and strong and healthy,

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:15.240
but I can't take physical exertion, so people look at me rather

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:19.430
funny when I'm walking along and she's carrying the bag,

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:22.750
or when we come up to the door and she lets me out,

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:26.410
and then she walks back, uhh.

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:29.260
Makes, makes it kind of embarrassing

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:34.090
and I attempt to cover that in many ways by stopping and resting,

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:36.460
or stopping and looking at something,

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:39.570
and just not seeing her doing these things.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:43.530
A lot of the times I do that. Bill has taken over a lot of this,

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:45.050
she has taken over a lot of it.

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:46.480
I don't do anything very much anymore

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:49.780
that requires physical exertion.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:54.460
That's probably one of the things that bothers me most.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:57.410
I could do a lot of these things at one time,

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:02.090
and now I can't and it's a rather frustrating experience.

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:06.490
It's sometimes disillusioning to think, well sure I can do this.

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:09.170
Then I find that I can't, I have to quit.

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:14.250
And this is one of the substitutes that I've found.

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:20.790
I make bedspreads and tablecloths, placemats, uh, afghans.

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:25.670
There are a lot of things can be done that would take away energy

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:28.600
that I expended in athletic contests,

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:31.820
or events prior to this that I now have to do something

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:36.530
that doesn't take that much physical exertion.

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:41.060
The absence of oxygen makes me tire very easily,

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:44.830
and many times I come home from school, put on my pajamas,

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:50.090
have dinner, go right to bed because that's the only energy that I have.

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:52.500
And by doing that I can teach.

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:54.430
Whereas if I tried to do a lot of other things,

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:57.020
then I couldn't, I'd just be completely out,

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:59.180
completely without energy.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:01.290
[Bill Sr.'s wife:] He's, he's learned to conserve himself

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:04.930
for the jobs that he wants to do and wants,

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:07.880
you know, enjoys doing and can do.

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:10.390
He spends many weekends in bed too,

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:11.560
but that's fine that,

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:15.430
by doing that, he's able to go to school and do his work, so...

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:20.350
[Narrator:] What does Bill suspect is the cause of his disease?

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:24.740
[Bill:] Possibly smoking and the fact that I, uhh,

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:26.780
well we don't know for sure.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:30.900
I had pneumonia twice and I smoked heavily,

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:33.390
so they don't know whether the smoking caused it

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:36.550
or whether the pneumonia caused me to have emphysema.

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:40.840
But the theory is the smoking, heavy smoking caused it.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:47.530
[Narrator:] Despite his father's condition and suspicion, Bill junior smokes.

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:51.360
[Bill Jr.:] I thought about quitting a couple times,

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:53.470
but uh, I never have gotten around to it,

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:59.050
and uh, I don't smoke in front of him often.

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:02.330
Sometimes when we play cards and we're together for long periods of time,

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:04.490
I'll have one in front of him,

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:07.450
but uh, I don't know sometimes,

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:09.610
when I've done something active

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:14.780
like I play football and bowl, things like that.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:16.930
And when I get short of breath,

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:19.380
it makes me wonder sometimes, you know,

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:21.590
am I headed for something like that?

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:25.000
And at those times particularly I think about quitting,

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:29.540
but uhh...in fact I did quit for about a month.

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:33.120
But I couldn't lose the desire.

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:35.190
[Interviewer:] How much do ya smoke?

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:37.940
[Bill Jr.:] Ohh anywhere from a half a pack to a pack a day.

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:41.790
I don't smoke heavily yet, and nowhere near as heavily

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:44.530
as he used to smoke.

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:47.170
[Bill Sr.'s wife:] I predict that he'll have it though.

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:51.530
I really think he will; he has some of the same symptoms already

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:52.820
that his father has.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:58.710
Sniffly nose and um, they both get sneezing spells, I'll call them,

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:00.740
and they'll sneeze 15 or 20 times,

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:03.630
and then they'll end up with a cold.

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:04.690
And Bill does that,

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:09.480
so he's very much like his father in a number of ways as you can see.

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:15.160
I really think he's uh, a good suspect.

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:15.970
[Interviewer:] Prospect.

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:17.340
[Bill Sr.'s wife:] Prospect then.

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:18.640
[Bill:] I wish he would quit,

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:21.950
the same way probably that my father wished that I would quit.

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:24.730
Uh I think I have more visible evidence, though,

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:29.780
of what it can do to ya than uh, I had from my father.

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:33.860
So I wish he would quit because I think it would be to his advantage,

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:35.360
not only from a health standpoint,

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:39.160
but from a monetary standpoint, because it's expensive to smoke,

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:42.280
as well as being a health hazard.

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:49.140
[Narrator:] Although there is no known cure for emphysema,

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:53.300
certain infrequent cases may be given some relief through surgery.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:55.290
Harry Krause is a case in point.

00:08:55.310 --> 00:08:57.860
He knew the operation was very risky,

00:08:57.880 --> 00:08:59.140
but felt he had nothing to lose.

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:01.340
[Harry:] I was no good to myself or anybody else.

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:04.960
I couldn't take a bath myself or anything else and uh,

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:07.250
I required help to do everything.

00:09:07.270 --> 00:09:12.170
And uh, uh it was a living death if you want to put it like that.

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:14.450
[Narrator:] According to Dr. Thomas D. Bartley,

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:16.580
the surgeon who performed the operation,

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:19.900
Harry Krause's specific condition warranted the surgery.

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:24.650
[Dr. Bartley:] A similar situation is depicted here in this whole lung section,

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:32.340
prepared at the research center in Jacksonville's Baptist Memorial Hospital.

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:38.440
The upper part of this lung has been destroyed by emphysema.

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:40.760
The lower parts...

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:49.390
uh, have disease in them as shown in these lightened areas here.

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:53.440
However, there is enough preservation of the normal architecture

00:09:53.460 --> 00:10:00.460
so that one could guess that removal of this part,

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:05.290
which is overinflated and compressing, or squashing,

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:14.290
the more normal areas, might result in improved function within this lung.

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:19.510
This of course occurred in Mr. Krause's situation,

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:25.460
and though he was totally disabled prior to his operation,

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:33.580
he has been able to return to a relatively normal level of activity.

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:36.140
[Narrator:] Although surgery did improve Harry's condition,

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:40.230
emphysema still forces him to lead a very limited life.

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:44.410
[Harry:] I turned the entire business setup over to her,

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:49.640
and, however, I am able to help in an advisory capacity,

00:10:49.660 --> 00:10:53.590
I can do no work at all, I can't lift anything.

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:55.540
Trying to agitates it,

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:58.020
and then you get clear down to where

00:10:58.040 --> 00:10:59.850
you can't even take care of yourself,

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:02.630
and it takes you a long time to build back up

00:11:02.650 --> 00:11:08.510
and get the uh stamina you would have if you didn't try.

00:11:08.530 --> 00:11:12.580
Walking too fast, too far tires you out.

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:17.770
Any carrying, even to the extent of five pounds in your hands

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:23.110
tires you out right now because in my condition right now it overloads me.

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:26.190
I can carry the weight that I've got if I'm very, very careful.

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:27.960
But if I add anything to it,

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:31.910
like a heavy overcoat or a big pair of boots or something like that,

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:35.400
it wears you out.

00:11:35.420 --> 00:11:41.720
[Harry's wife:] Oh, you have to do, do a lot of extra work.

00:11:41.740 --> 00:11:50.480
Things that he can't do and all the taking care of him when he is down.

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:52.140
[Narrator:] To live with emphysema,

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:54.960
the Krauses have had to make many adjustments.

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:57.010
[Harry's wife:] You just don't go anymore, or do things

00:11:57.030 --> 00:11:59.980
that you used to do for pleasure, you know.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.660
It's stay home practically all the time.

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:06.440
[Harry:] You don't have any social life.

00:12:06.460 --> 00:12:11.370
All the friends that you had, you used to go out with in the evening and things like that,

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:14.750
they drift away knowing the condition and everything.

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:16.320
You have friends that drop in on you,

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:18.960
and your close relations stick by you,

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:22.340
but uh, that's the extent of your social life.

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:24.700
You lose all contact

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:30.510
because you just can't go in public places with anything like this.

00:12:30.530 --> 00:12:32.090
The doctors advise against it

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:38.800
because colds are transmitted and if I get one, I'm in trouble.

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:41.030
[Narrator:] For a man who formerly led an active life,

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:44.770
slowing down to almost a standstill has its obvious problems.

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:46.500
[Harry:] Yes I was active all my life.

00:12:46.520 --> 00:12:49.740
In fact, we built this place here ourselves and uh,

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:52.570
all the hard work that's in it, we put in it,

00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:56.520
and uh we've been that way all of our lives.

00:12:56.540 --> 00:13:04.200
We worked hard and had to work, you might say, for what we got.

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:07.250
That's the hardest thing for somebody that's got a lot of ambition,

00:13:07.270 --> 00:13:10.200
to keep from doing, is overdoing with something like this,

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:13.380
because if you overdo it,

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:16.840
you set yourself back to where you can't even get around.

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:21.770
You just gotta quit everything and go in complete rest.

00:13:21.790 --> 00:13:23.080
[Narrator:] In his battle to breathe,

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:25.160
Harry must go through an exhausting effort

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:31.080
to clear the fluid from his respiratory system four times each day.

00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:34.890
[Harry:] We take the machine for approximately a half an hour

00:13:34.910 --> 00:13:39.870
and that has medicine in it and we fill our lungs with that machine,

00:13:39.890 --> 00:13:42.950
and then after that we get down on the floor

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:46.590
and my wife pushes on my one side and then the other side,

00:13:46.610 --> 00:13:50.050
and then my back.

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:52.290
[Interviewer:] So what was the cause of  emphysema?

00:13:52.310 --> 00:13:56.450
[Harry:] Well, I believe the cause, I'm sure the cause was cigarettes.

00:13:57.480 --> 00:13:59.440
[Interviewer:] How much did you smoke?

00:13:59.470 --> 00:14:02.810
[Harry:] Well, at one time I smoked about four packs a day,

00:14:02.830 --> 00:14:06.430
and towards the last it was two regular.

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:08.210
I guess that's partly my trouble,

00:14:08.230 --> 00:14:10.640
if I hadn't smoked at that time probably,

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:13.120
it wouldn't have been as bad on me.

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:14.700
But you just don't do those things,

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:20.710
of course in those days you didn't know what cigarettes was gonna do to you in later life,

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:24.390
but uh now they're beginning to find out.

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:25.870
[Narrator:] For Frank Ussery,

00:14:25.890 --> 00:14:30.170
almost totally incapacitated by emphysema for the past four years,

00:14:30.190 --> 00:14:34.440
the routine task of shaving is a major physical effort.

00:14:34.460 --> 00:14:38.770
[Frank:] Well, I just get out of breath soon as I'm shaving...

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:42.460
and uh, I don't have the strength to sit down,

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:44.420
to stand up to shave, I give out,

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:46.220
my legs give out on me.

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:47.590
And I sit on that stool,

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:51.350
but it's not so comfortable, guess it's too high or something.

00:14:51.370 --> 00:14:53.900
And of course if I take my arms

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:57.490
and lay 'em on the sink or something and use one hand at a time,

00:14:57.510 --> 00:14:59.330
it's not quite so bad,

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:05.240
but it takes strength, just moving my arms around.

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:06.750
It takes strength and...

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:09.300
I don't have too much of it,

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:11.760
I guess, I get tired.

00:15:11.990 --> 00:15:14.360
I...while I'm shaving I take time once in a while

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:19.920
and rest for a few seconds, ten, 15, 20 seconds, 30 seconds,

00:15:19.940 --> 00:15:20.880
and I start again,

00:15:20.900 --> 00:15:23.220
and I get by with it that way.

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:27.610
Exertion takes all my strength away from me.

00:15:27.630 --> 00:15:30.290
[Interviewer:] What, for instance, what kind of exertion?

00:15:30.310 --> 00:15:33.340
[Frank:] Well walking, walking,

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:36.330
mostly just walking, 'cause that's about all I do.

00:15:36.350 --> 00:15:37.770
I wouldn't have any energy enough

00:15:37.790 --> 00:15:40.330
to do any kind of work.

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:44.910
Uh I don't even write any letters anymore.

00:15:44.930 --> 00:15:47.260
Dressing tires me, stooping over,

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:50.480
to put on my socks and my shoes.

00:15:50.500 --> 00:15:53.420
Standing up, putting on my pants and bending down,

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:55.160
putting my shirt on.

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:58.310
Well uh if I do it, if I do it slowly,

00:15:58.330 --> 00:16:01.770
I can gradually go ahead, probably take me three or four five minutes

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:04.390
to get my pants and my shirt on

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:07.380
and then I won't be out of breath when I get it on.

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:11.850
But then when I walk from uh, the bedroom into the kitchen uh,

00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:15.150
I couldn't begin to start drinking coffee right then

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:16.320
because my breath is so short.

00:16:16.340 --> 00:16:17.400
I just haven't got the breath

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:22.650
and that, that's the way, it tires me to eat even,

00:16:22.670 --> 00:16:26.360
particularly eating fast, to try to chew fast.

00:16:26.380 --> 00:16:30.170
It actually tires me to talk too long.

00:16:30.190 --> 00:16:32.560
Well, I worry most that I can't get out and do

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:35.010
the things I like to do.

00:16:35.030 --> 00:16:38.550
Just cooped up in the house all the time.

00:16:38.570 --> 00:16:41.190
I can't even drive a car anymore.

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:42.410
Even with power steering,

00:16:42.430 --> 00:16:44.150
I don't have the strength.

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:45.670
Even jostling up and down in the car,

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:48.290
it tires me out.

00:16:48.310 --> 00:16:52.570
[Frank's wife:] We've always enjoyed going on trips so much

00:16:52.590 --> 00:16:58.930
and we all used to love to visit our neighbors,

00:16:58.950 --> 00:17:01.240
and now that's impossible.

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:03.960
We don't even get to go to church together.

00:17:03.980 --> 00:17:05.830
I have to go to the church service in the morning,

00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:10.880
and then come home and listen to the 11 o'clock service with Frank.

00:17:10.900 --> 00:17:12.580
Just seeing him this way

00:17:12.600 --> 00:17:16.190
is just the most tragic thing to me.

00:17:16.210 --> 00:17:21.760
But uh, and then I used to depend on him so much to do things for me,

00:17:21.780 --> 00:17:24.270
and now he can't even dry dishes.

00:17:24.290 --> 00:17:28.550
And uh, I think that's been a lot in our lives.

00:17:28.570 --> 00:17:32.720
It's just the idea he just cannot do things for himself,

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:37.040
or for me, either one, or for anybody else.

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:40.440
And he's always been so active and fished and hunted

00:17:40.460 --> 00:17:44.740
and everything like that but now he just...

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:46.520
He loves to read and look at the TV,

00:17:46.540 --> 00:17:51.070
but that's about the only thing, recreation he gets.

00:17:51.090 --> 00:17:52.710
[Frank:] If I should want to go get in the car

00:17:52.730 --> 00:17:53.680
and walk to the car,

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:57.540
if I walk real slow, I do a whole lot better.

00:17:57.560 --> 00:17:58.570
If I try walk fast to the car,

00:17:58.590 --> 00:17:59.550
by the time I get out there

00:17:59.570 --> 00:18:02.940
I'd be so completely out of breath I can hardly catch my breath.

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:07.140
[Narrator:] Frank's condition causes his wife a great deal of apprehension.

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:09.950
[Frank's wife:] And then it's always so frightening to me at night

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:12.900
when I'm here all by myself with him

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:16.520
and I'm always so afraid he's going to get sick during the night.

00:18:16.540 --> 00:18:22.320
But so far he's done pretty well about that.

00:18:22.340 --> 00:18:25.560
But uh, I think that's enough,

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:30.610
and it's been worrying me, it's just the idea that

00:18:30.630 --> 00:18:34.070
he might need me during the night and I wouldn't know it.

00:18:34.090 --> 00:18:38.950
[Narrator:] Frank, too, cites cigarettes as the probable cause of his emphysema.

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:41.000
[Frank:] I was smoking cigarettes,

00:18:41.020 --> 00:18:44.290
and I guess like 90 percent I might say, these cigarettes,

00:18:44.310 --> 00:18:48.790
I guess caused me to be short breath, but I wouldn't quit smoking.

00:18:48.810 --> 00:18:57.470
Probably like an average man would, I don't know.

00:18:57.490 --> 00:18:59.500
[Nurse:] Blow out your breath, keep it out.

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:03.070
[Narrator:] There is still a great deal to be learned about emphysema.

00:19:03.090 --> 00:19:07.860
At the University of Florida Medical Center and other hospitals throughout the country,

00:19:07.880 --> 00:19:09.270
intensive research is underway

00:19:09.290 --> 00:19:11.150
to develop a cure for this disease,

00:19:11.400 --> 00:19:16.660
whose victims are increasing in alarming proportions.

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:20.660
[Doctor:] Faster.

00:19:20.680 --> 00:19:23.620
[Narrator:] Social security disability payments to emphysema patients

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:28.960
rank second only to those made to people disabled by heart disease.

00:19:30.750 --> 00:19:32.230
Under laboratory conditions,

00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:36.370
the effect of emphysema on work capacity can be carefully measured.

00:19:37.670 --> 00:19:42.300
[Doctor:] We're following Mr. Ussery's heart rate and breathing rate

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:45.810
as he recovers from an exercise load.

00:19:45.830 --> 00:19:50.610
His ability to exercise against a known load

00:19:50.630 --> 00:19:55.380
is approximately half a normal person's ability.

00:19:55.400 --> 00:20:02.830
His emphysema has caused this and is shown by a rapid breathing rate

00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:07.770
even three to five minutes after he stopped pedaling.

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:14.560
[Doctor:] ...Emphysema such as Mr. Ussery has is a disease of the lungs

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:21.120
caused by bronchitis, a narrowing of the small airways.

00:20:21.610 --> 00:20:26.980
And also a destruction of the lung tissue itself.

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:32.590
The bronchitis, or the obstruction part, causes the lungs to overinflate.

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:36.500
They balloon up so that a patient is forced to breathe

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:40.640
on top of them, so to speak.

00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:45.700
If we look here, we can see how a normal person breathes quietly

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:48.830
and then when he takes in a deep breath,

00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:55.040
he can bring in up to three liters or over three quarts of air,

00:20:55.060 --> 00:20:58.830
and blow out rapidly almost five.

00:20:58.850 --> 00:21:05.070
But a person with emphysema is starting at a high point in his lung volume

00:21:05.090 --> 00:21:10.110
and he can only take in one or two quarts.

00:21:10.130 --> 00:21:16.710
And worst of all, he can blow out only slowly two or three quarts of air.

00:21:17.820 --> 00:21:22.710
Now the diffusion, the way the tissue of the lungs take up oxygen

00:21:22.730 --> 00:21:25.140
and put it into the bloodstream

00:21:25.160 --> 00:21:30.200
that courses through the lungs from the air that a person breathes,

00:21:30.220 --> 00:21:36.030
is important and a normal person's value might be 20.

00:21:36.050 --> 00:21:41.470
A patient such as Mr. Ussery has destroyed much of his lung tissue

00:21:41.490 --> 00:21:45.130
and can take up only half as much as a normal person can.

00:21:45.150 --> 00:21:49.520
When a person can't breathe deeply and can't exhale rapidly,

00:21:49.540 --> 00:21:52.670
he becomes desperately short of breath.

00:21:52.690 --> 00:21:54.410
He can't work,

00:21:54.430 --> 00:21:59.490
he is in trouble at home just getting around the house.

00:21:59.510 --> 00:22:05.600
His ability to support his family is ruined.

00:22:05.620 --> 00:22:10.230
We can't cure emphysema; we can help patients that have it.

00:22:10.250 --> 00:22:12.230
They should not give up hope.

00:22:12.250 --> 00:22:17.290
We can get them to quit smoking

00:22:17.310 --> 00:22:23.380
and guarantee a certain return in their ability to work.

00:22:23.400 --> 00:22:27.380
We can also use medicine such as adrenaline

00:22:27.400 --> 00:22:33.860
for them to breathe and dilate those small bronchial tubes.

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:36.420
We give them exercises for posture

00:22:36.440 --> 00:22:42.060
and to help them bring up the secretions out of their lungs.

00:22:42.080 --> 00:22:47.020
[Narrator:] Many health authorities still refer to emphysema's cause as unknown.

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:48.310
Increasingly however,

00:22:48.330 --> 00:22:50.060
researchers are placing the lion's share

00:22:50.080 --> 00:22:53.740
of the blame on a single source.

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:59.620
[Doctor:] The important way to get rid of emphysema is not treatment,

00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:05.210
it's to teach children that the long-term effect of cigarette smoking

00:23:05.230 --> 00:23:08.430
is very dangerous to their health.

00:23:08.540 --> 00:23:14.140
[Sound of man using breathing machine, coughing, and wife pounding on his back.]

00:23:14.150 --> 00:23:20.850
[Narrator:] Mark Twain once observed that each person is born with one possession which outvalues all his others.

00:23:20.870 --> 00:23:22.940
His last breath.

00:23:22.960 --> 00:23:26.280
Emphysema victims have gone Mark Twain one better.

00:23:26.300 --> 00:23:30.590
In their day-in day-out battle to keep their lungs working,

00:23:30.610 --> 00:23:34.910
they have come to cherish not just their last breath

00:23:34.930 --> 00:23:36.470
but their every breath.

00:23:36.470 --> 00:23:46.450
[Man coughs, woman pounds on his back, breathing machine whirs.]

00:23:50.300 --> 00:25:07.380
[Music]

